# CSS Flexbox — Flexbox Froggy

**Practice:** Completed all 24 levels  
**Time taken:** Approximately 1 hour  
**Method:** Hands-on problem-solving

## 1. Flex Container

```css
.container {
  display: flex;
}
```

- `display: flex` creates a flex container.
- Its direct children become flex items.
- Most Flexbox layout properties are applied to the parent container.

## 2. Flex Direction

```css
flex-direction: row;
flex-direction: column;
flex-direction: row-reverse;
flex-direction: column-reverse;
```

Controls the main axis and the direction in which flex items are laid out.

- `row`: Horizontal, left to right in the usual left-to-right layout.
- `column`: Vertical, top to bottom.
- `row-reverse`: Horizontal, reversed.
- `column-reverse`: Vertical, reversed.

Default: `row`.

## 3. Justify Content

```css
justify-content: flex-start;
justify-content: flex-end;
justify-content: center;
justify-content: space-between;
justify-content: space-around;
justify-content: space-evenly;
```

Controls alignment and distribution along the **main axis**.

- `flex-start`: Items toward the start.
- `flex-end`: Items toward the end.
- `center`: Items grouped in the center.
- `space-between`: Equal space between items, with no extra space at the ends.
- `space-around`: Space around each item.
- `space-evenly`: Equal spacing between items and the container edges.

The main axis depends on `flex-direction`.

## 4. Align Items

```css
align-items: flex-start;
align-items: flex-end;
align-items: center;
align-items: stretch;
align-items: baseline;
```

Controls alignment along the **cross axis** within each flex line.

- `flex-start`: Align items toward the cross-axis start.
- `flex-end`: Align items toward the cross-axis end.
- `center`: Center items along the cross axis.
- `stretch`: Stretch items when applicable.
- `baseline`: Align text baselines.

## 5. Flex Wrap

```css
flex-wrap: nowrap;
flex-wrap: wrap;
flex-wrap: wrap-reverse;
```

Controls whether flex items can move onto additional flex lines.

- `nowrap`: Keeps items on one flex line; items may shrink if permitted.
- `wrap`: Allows items to move onto additional lines.
- `wrap-reverse`: Allows wrapping while reversing the cross-axis direction of the flex lines.

With `flex-direction: row`, wrapping usually creates additional rows. With `flex-direction: column`, it usually creates additional columns.

## 6. Align Content

```css
align-content: flex-start;
align-content: flex-end;
align-content: center;
align-content: space-between;
align-content: space-around;
align-content: space-evenly;
align-content: stretch;
```

Controls the alignment and distribution of **multiple flex lines** along the cross axis when space is available.

Its line-distribution effect requires multiple flex lines.

Do not confuse it with `align-items`:
- `align-items`: Aligns items within a flex line.
- `align-content`: Aligns or distributes the flex lines themselves.

## 7. Align Self

```css
align-self: center;
```

Overrides `align-items` for an individual flex item.

It is applied to the item, not the flex container.

## 8. Key Takeaways

- `justify-content` → main axis.
- `align-items` → cross-axis alignment of items within a flex line.
- `align-content` → distribution of multiple flex lines.
- `align-self` → alignment override for an individual flex item.
- `flex-wrap` → whether items can wrap into additional flex lines.
- `flex-direction` → determines the main axis and item direction.
- Flexbox properties must be understood in relation to the container's available space and axis direction.

## Practice Result

Completed all 24 Flexbox Froggy levels in approximately one hour.

The main learning outcome was understanding how direction, wrapping, and alignment properties work together to position items.
